1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,463
Open the CE lab and use the 2N3904 common-emitter circuit.

2
00:00:05,663 --> 00:00:12,090
This teaching example uses forty-seven and twenty-four kilo-ohms in the base divider, an eight
point

3
00:00:12,090 --> 00:00:15,946
two kilo-ohm collector resistor, and a plus fifteen-volt supply.

4
00:00:16,146 --> 00:00:18,855
Use your own selected values for the assignment.

5
00:00:19,755 --> 00:00:26,582
Use a ten microfarad input coupling capacitor, an eight hundred twenty-ohm R4, a three point
three

6
00:00:26,582 --> 00:00:31,702
kilo-ohm R5, and a one hundred microfarad bypass capacitor for this example.

7
00:00:31,902 --> 00:00:34,206
Connect the input through CIN to the base.

8
00:00:34,406 --> 00:00:36,518
Measure VIN before that capacitor.

9
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:40,511
Connect the bypass capacitor across R5 only.

10
00:00:40,711 --> 00:00:44,594
R4 remains unbypassed and helps control the signal gain.

11
00:00:44,794 --> 00:00:51,265
On a physical build, check transistor pinout and capacitor polarity using the actual component

12
00:00:51,265 --> 00:00:51,727
documentation.

13
00:00:51,927 --> 00:00:55,149
Then check the breadboard against the schematic before power on.

14
00:00:56,049 --> 00:00:59,441
Open the DC check with the input signal disabled.

15
00:00:59,641 --> 00:01:03,715
Record the base, emitter, and collector voltages and collector current.

16
00:01:03,915 --> 00:01:09,163
A useful initial active-region check is collector above base, and base above emitter.

17
00:01:09,363 --> 00:01:14,078
Also consider whether the collector has enough room for the intended output swing.

18
00:01:14,978 --> 00:01:21,242
This captured example uses a two hundred millivolt peak sine input at one kilohertz, with zero

19
00:01:21,242 --> 00:01:21,634
offset.

20
00:01:21,834 --> 00:01:24,245
That is zero point four volts peak-to-peak.

21
00:01:24,445 --> 00:01:28,306
Set CH1 to VIN and CH2 to VC, the collector.

22
00:01:28,506 --> 00:01:31,215
Both references connect to zero-volt ground.

23
00:01:32,115 --> 00:01:34,697
Press Run and read the numerical measurements.

24
00:01:34,897 --> 00:01:40,967
The capture shows about zero point four volts peak-to-peak in and three point eight four out,
giving

25
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:43,110
about minus nine point six gain.

26
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:47,961
A smooth-looking trace alone does not prove active operation throughout the cycle.

27
00:01:48,861 --> 00:01:54,394
If the output clips, or the collector falls below the base during part of the cycle, reduce the

28
00:01:54,394 --> 00:01:55,623
input and check again.

29
00:01:55,823 --> 00:01:59,578
One hundred millivolts peak is a useful smaller-signal trial.

30
00:01:59,778 --> 00:02:04,279
Report the limit you observe instead of forcing the four-volt peak-to-peak target.

31
00:02:05,179 --> 00:02:08,081
Repeat at ten kilohertz with the same input first.

32
00:02:08,281 --> 00:02:12,910
Record both channel amplitudes, signed gain, and the actual input setting.

33
00:02:13,110 --> 00:02:16,737
Save the circuit, your breadboard image, and labeled waveforms.

34
00:02:16,937 --> 00:02:21,182
Download the CE report and submit it separately from the CS report.
